>
  <p>Everyone tells you to "just start", which is true and useless. The reason you cannot just start is that the task feels bad before you begin: boring, hard, or likely to show you are not as good at it as you hoped. Procrastination is how you escape that feeling. Fix the feeling and the behaviour follows.</p>

  <h2 id="why">Why you procrastinate</h2>
  <p>Piers Steel's review of hundreds of studies found procrastination is strongest when a task feels unpleasant, when the reward is far away, and when you doubt you can do it well. None of that is about time management. It is about mood in the moment. Once you see that, the useful question changes from "how do I force myself?" to "how do I make starting feel smaller and the payoff feel closer?"</p>

  <h2 id="methods">9 methods that work</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Shrink the first step.</strong> Not "write the essay" but "open the document and write the title". A step you cannot refuse is the whole point.</li>
    <li><strong>Use the two-minute rule.</strong> Promise yourself two minutes. You can stop after. You rarely will.</li>
    <li><strong>Plan the when and where.</strong> "After dinner, at my desk, I revise biology for 25 minutes." These if-then plans roughly double follow-through in Gollwitzer's research.</li>
    <li><strong>Make the easy option harder.</strong> Phone in another room, browser tabs closed, notifications off. You will not resist what is within arm's reach for three hours straight.</li>
    <li><strong>Time-box with a timer.</strong> A 25-minute <a href="/tools/pomodoro-timer/">Pomodoro</a> turns "study all night" into one short, finishable round.</li>
    <li><strong>Temptation bundling.</strong> Pair the task with something you like: a favourite playlist, your good coffee, a specific cafe.</li>
    <li><strong>Lower the standard for the first draft.</strong> Give yourself permission to do it badly first. You can fix bad; you cannot fix blank.</li>
    <li><strong>Forgive the last slip.</strong> Students who forgave themselves for procrastinating on one exam procrastinated less on the next. Guilt keeps the cycle going.</li>
    <li><strong>Work where others work.</strong> A library or a quiet study group raises the social cost of scrolling.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="plan">Build it into a plan</h2>
  <p>Methods fade without a structure to hang them on. Decide your study blocks for the week in advance so you are not negotiating with yourself every evening. Our <a href="/tools/study-plan-generator/">study plan generator</a> turns your subjects and exam date into dated blocks, and the <a href="/tools/assignment-planner/">assignment planner</a> breaks a big task into small steps with their own due dates, so the first step is always obvious. For deeper habits, see <a href="/guides/how-to-stay-motivated-to-study/">how to stay motivated to study</a> and <a href="/guides/how-to-build-a-daily-study-routine/">how to build a daily study routine</a>.</p>
